MongoDB Installation Process and Types

-----------------------------------------------

===> 1. MongoDB Installation Process

===> A. Prerequisites
- Operating System: Windows, macOS, Linux (Ubuntu, CentOS, Debian, etc.)
- Suitable hardware resources
- Administrative privileges

-----------------------------------------------

===> B. Installation Steps

For Windows:

1. Download MongoDB:
   - Visit the official MongoDB Download Center: [https://www.mongodb.com/try/download/community](https://www.mongodb.com/try/download/community)
   - Choose the version compatible with your Windows OS.
   - Download the MSI installer.

2. Run the Installer:
   - Double-click the downloaded `.msi` file.
   - Follow the setup wizard:
     - Choose *Complete* or *Custom* installation.
     - Select the install location.
   - When prompted, choose to install MongoDB as a Windows service (recommended).

3. Configure MongoDB:
   - Create a directory for data storage (e.g., `C:\data\db`).
   - You can set this in the configuration if using custom paths.

4. Start MongoDB:
   - The service starts automatically if installed as a service.
   - Alternatively, run `clear
   ` from the command line.

5. Verify Installation:
   - Open Command Prompt.
   - Type `mongo` to connect to the MongoDB shell.

-----------------------------------------------

For macOS:

1. Using Homebrew:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   brew tap mongodb/brew
   brew install mongodb-community
   ------------------------####-----------------------

2. Start MongoDB:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   brew services start mongodb-community
   ------------------------####-----------------------

3. Verify:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   mongo
   ------------------------####-----------------------

-----------------------------------------------

For Linux (Ubuntu):

1. Import the public key:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   wget -qO - https://www.mongodb.org/static/pgp/server-6.0.asc | sudo apt-key add -
   ------------------------####-----------------------

2. Create the list file:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   echo "deb [ arch=amd64,arm64 ] https://repo.mongodb.org/apt/ubuntu $(lsb_release -cs)/mongodb-org/6.0 multiverse" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mongodb-org-6.0.list
   ------------------------####-----------------------

3. Update packages:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   sudo apt update
   ------------------------####-----------------------

4. Install MongoDB:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   sudo apt install -y mongodb-org
   ------------------------####-----------------------

5. Start MongoDB:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   sudo systemctl start mongod
   sudo systemctl enable mongod
   ------------------------####-----------------------

6. Verify:
   ------------------------terminal-----------------------
   mongo
   ------------------------####-----------------------

-----------------------------------------------

===> 2. Types of MongoDB

MongoDB offers various deployment types based on use cases:


=> Community Edition 
    Description-> Free, open-source version of MongoDB 
    Use Case->Development, testing, small-scale production 

=> Enterprise Edition 
    Description-> Paid version with additional features, security, and support 
    Use Case-> Large-scale production, enterprise use, compliance 

=> MongoDB Atlas 
    Description->Fully managed cloud database service 
    Use Case->Cloud deployment, ease of management, scalability 

=> MongoDB on-premises 
    Description->Self-hosted deployment on local infrastructure 
    Use Case->Custom control, data residency requirements 

-----------------------------------------------

===> Summary

- Installation involves downloading the appropriate package for your OS, following setup steps, and starting the MongoDB server.
- Types include Community, Enterprise, Atlas (cloud), and on-premises deployments, catering to different needs.

